{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Firstborn of his ox, etc.</b> Here he blessed two great ones who come forth from Joseph. Concerning Menasseh the firstborn he said: ״בְּכוֹר שׁוֹרוֹ הָדָר לוֹ״ — an allusion to Gideon. He likened him to the ox, alluding to what the angel of HaShem said to Gideon (Judges 6:25): ״קַח אֶת פַּר הַשּׁוֹר אֲשֶׁר לְאָבִיךָ וְגוֹ׳ וְלָקַחְתָּ אֶת הַפָּר הַשֵּׁנִי וְהַעֲלִיתָ עוֹלָה״, etc." "<b>And His saying</b> הָדָר לוֹ — as it says there (Judges 7:2): ״וַיֹּאמֶר ה׳ אֶל גִּדְעוֹן רַב הָעָם אֲשֶׁר אִתָּךְ מִתִּתִּי אֶת מִדְיָן בְּיָדָם פֶּן יִתְפָּאֵר עָלַי יִשְׂרָאֵל לֵאמֹר יָדִי הוֹשִׁיעָה לִּי״. And this glory — it is the הָדָר mentioned here: that not many from Israel went up, but only a few men, and thereby the salvation would be הָדָר לוֹ — glorious to the deliverer. And that is what it says: הָדָר לוֹ." "<b>And his horns are the horns of a wild ox.</b> This corresponds to Joshua, who comes from Ephraim, whose fame was throughout all the earth, for HaShem magnified him and exalted his greatness like the horns of a wild ox: ״בָּהֶם עַמִּים יְנַגַּח״, etc. For when the kings of the Amorites heard what HaShem had done with him, no spirit remained in them to stand before him." "<b>And he returned</b> and Moses explained his words: the two blessings he spoke correspond to the two tribes, and that is what it says: ״וְהֵם רִבְבוֹת אֶפְרַיִם וְהֵם אַלְפֵי מְנַשֶּׁה״. And although in the blessing he first said ״בְּכוֹר שׁוֹרוֹ״ — which corresponds to Menasseh — because his blessing is smaller than the blessing of Ephraim, he began with the lesser to add and ascend in the blessing. But when he mentioned them, he put the blessing of Ephraim first according to their greatness, for the greatness of Joshua is greater than the greatness of Gideon."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:22:20.696856660Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}